Patch 6500 (rc-SPU pass 1): the WWS job code-buffer slot is a property of the policy module, not a runtime constant.

A WWS policy module keeps the LS address of the job code buffer in a fixed local-store slot and enters a job at
LS[slot] + u32@(job header + 0x10). The slot is 0x1320 in the LBP and Killzone 3 policy modules, hard-coded in nine
places of the runtime, but 0x1330 in Ratchet & Clank: Tools of Destruction's (policy module EA 0x10053d00:
`lqa $r2,0x1330` @LS 0x3024, `stqa $r3,0x1330` @LS 0x21AC; it stores an unrelated LS pointer at 0x1320 and never
reads it back). With the literal 0x1320 the overlay match in spu_indirect_branch reads the wrong word on ToD, never
marks a lifted job module resident, and every job falls to the interpreter (patch 1069's path).
Now: g_spu_wws_code_buf_slot[] indexed by the policy module's image id (0 = default 0x1320, so ports that register
nothing are unchanged), spu_wws_set_code_buffer_slot(pm_image_id, lsa) for a port's SPU registration,
spu_wws_code_buffer_slot(image_id), spu_wws_code_buffer(ctx) and spu_wws_resident_job_image(ctx, &cb) (also bounds the
16-byte header read without the u32 wrap of `codeBase + 16 <= SPU_LS_SIZE`). Sites: spu_channels.c overlay match
(functional), frame trace, two diagnostics; spu_interp.c histogram key; spu_context.h both write-probe gates;
spurs_policy.c [pm-end]. spu_pm_ctx_describe keeps its raw 0x1320/0x1330 columns (labelled by address).
Test: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_wws_code_slot.c (host, CI linux gcc/clang + macos). General; upstream only with the
PM's OK.

